package cajeta.lang;

/**
 * The universal root of the cajeta class hierarchy.
 *
 * Every class **implicitly extends `Object`** (via the auto-extend
 * pass in `CajetaLlvmVisitor::visitClassDeclaration`), so the three
 * methods declared here are inherited by every user class unless
 * manually overridden. See
 * [specification/lang/Lang.md § Object](../../../../../docs/specification/lang/Lang.md)
 * for the full design.
 *
 * ## Default behaviour
 *
 * | Method      | Default                                                    |
 * |-------------|------------------------------------------------------------|
 * | `hash()`    | **pointer-identity**, mixed with the per-process seed     |
 * | `toString()`| `null` placeholder until the `String` surface stabilizes  |
 * | `clone()`   | `null` placeholder until ownership semantics stabilize    |
 *
 * **Identity, not structural.** `Object.hash()` returns the object's
 * heap address mixed through SplitMix64 with the per-process seed —
 * matching Java's `Object.hashCode()` shape, not Rust's `derive(Hash)`.
 * Two distinct instances with identical field values produce different
 * hashes by default. Classes that need value-keyed semantics
 * (`HashMap``<Point, V>` where `Point(1, 2)` should equal `Point(1, 2)`)
 * **override `hash()` manually**, or opt into structural synthesis
 * via the `@AutoHash` annotation (planned — see §"Auto-hash opt-in"
 * in specification/lang/Lang.md).
 *
 * The per-process seed is initialized from OS entropy at process
 * start; hash values are stable for the value's lifetime within a
 * process but not across restarts. That kills hash-flooding attacks
 * against `HashMap` keyed on user-controlled input.
 *
 * ## Example
 *
 * ```cajeta
 * // Override hash() for value-keyed semantics instead of pointer identity.
 * public class Point {
 *     stack int64 x;
 *     stack int64 y;
 *
 *     public int64 hash() {
 *         return (x << 32) ^ y;   // structural, not heap-address derived
 *     }
 * }
 *
 * // operator== is inherited from Object as `this.hash() == other.hash()`,
 * // so once hash() is value-keyed a `HashMap` treats Point(1, 2) as a
 * // single bucket and the auto-derived `!=` follows for free.
 * ```
 *
 * ## Deferred items
 *
 * - `operator==(Object)` — needs the `@Native` bridge to handle
 *   boolean (`i1`) return-type coercion before its native stub can
 *   ship. Until then, identity equality on class references uses
 *   `Hash``.identity` and manual override is the path for structural
 *   equality.
 * - `@AutoHash` annotation — opt-in structural-hash synthesis for
 *   value-keyed classes. Synthesizer-generated diagnostics must
 *   attribute every failure (unhashable field type, transitive
 *   cycle, etc.) to the **annotated class + offending field name**
 *   so the user can fix it without sifting through generated code.
 *
 * ## See also
 *
 * `Hash` — identity/combine helpers and the per-process seed.
 * `DefaultHasher` — the streaming hasher value-keyed classes feed.
 */
public class Object {

    /**
     * Returns a 64-bit identity hash for this object.
     *
     * The default implementation is **pointer-identity** — the
     * object's heap address mixed through the SplitMix64 finalizer
     * with the per-process seed, same path as
     * `Hash``.identity(this)`. Two distinct instances with
     * identical field values produce different hashes; this is the
     * `Object.hashCode()` shape, not `derive(Hash)`.
     *
     * Override manually for value-keyed semantics, or apply
     * `@AutoHash` for compiler-synthesized structural hashing across
     * the class's fields.
     *
     * Subclasses that override `hash()` **must** also override
     * `operator==()` once it lands, and vice versa — the
     * "equal values hash equally" contract.
     *
     * @returns 64-bit identity hash, well-distributed for use as a
     *          `HashMap``<K, V>` / `HashSet``<T>` bucket key.
     * @Complexity O(1).
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_object_hash")
    public int64 hash();

    /**
     * Returns a `String` representation of this object suitable for
     * debug output.
     *
     * The default implementation returns `null` until both the
     * structural synthesizer lands and the `String`
     * construction surface stabilizes. The synthesized form will
     * follow the `TypeName(field1=value1, field2=value2, ...)` shape —
     * close to Rust's `#[derive(Debug)]`, sufficient for `println(x)`
     * during development.
     *
     * Override manually to provide a user-facing representation —
     * the synthesized form is intentionally debug-shaped, not
     * presentation-quality.
     *
     * @returns debug-shaped string, or `null` until the synthesizer
     *          lands.
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_object_to_string")
    public String toString();

    /**
     * Returns a shallow copy of this object (slice-spec §6.4 — the
     * explicit sibling of the implicit value-copy). Live via the RTTI
     * field walk in `__cajeta_object_clone`:
     *
     * - **value-typed fields** (primitives, structs, enums) — copied
     *   by `memcpy` of the bits
     * - **String fields** — a fresh stake on the same immutable byte
     *   buffer (wrapper-per-stake; cajeta has no GC, so aliasing one
     *   wrapper would use-after-free when either owner drops)
     * - **other class-typed fields** — copied by reference (Java's
     *   shallow `Object.clone()`); override to deep-copy when that's
     *   the right thing
     * - a **String receiver** DETACHES: the (possibly windowed) text
     *   materializes into a fresh owned buffer — the retention-
     *   amplification valve (slice-spec §4.4)
     *
     * Returns ownership (`#Object`) — the caller owns the copy.
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_object_clone")
    public #Object clone();

    /**
     * Equality operator — true iff `this` and `other` hash equal.
     *
     * Default implementation: `this.hash() == other.hash()`. Because
     * `hash()` is virtual, the semantics shift by overriding hash()
     * alone:
     *
     *   - Object (default identity hash): pointer-derived hash via
     *     splitmix64 — distinct pointers hash to distinct values
     *     (splitmix64 is bijective on the pointer ⊕ seed input), so
     *     `a == b` IS pointer identity in practice.
     *   - `String` (content hash): UTF-8-derived hash via SipHash-1-3
     *     (planned) — `a == b` IS value equality. `String` does NOT
     *     override operator==; it only overrides hash(), and the
     *     virtual dispatch through this method body carries the
     *     semantic shift.
     *
     * Hash-collision caveat: for value-based hashes, two distinct
     * values could hash equal at probability ~2^-64. For
     * collision-sensitive semantics (cryptographic equality, exact
     * legal-text comparison), use an explicit byte-by-byte comparison
     * method rather than `==`.
     *
     * Returns boolean. The companion `!=` is auto-derived from this
     * method (no explicit operator!= declaration needed) — the
     * compiler synthesizes `a != b` as the negation of `a == b`.
     */
    public static boolean operator== (Object a, Object b) {
        // Null-safe: a reference may be null (an unset field, a NIL tree
        // leaf, an absent map entry). Comparing against the `null` literal
        // lowers to a pointer `icmp` — NOT back through this method — so the
        // guard is non-recursive. Two nulls are equal; one null is not. Only
        // when both are non-null do we dispatch hash() (which would otherwise
        // load a vtable from a null pointer and segfault).
        if (a == null || b == null) {
            return a == null && b == null;
        }
        return a.hash() == b.hash();
    }

    /**
     * **Virtual destructor — the root of every class's drop.**
     *
     * Every class implicitly extends `Object`, so destruction is **virtual**:
     * dropping a value through *any* base reference dispatches to the
     * most-derived destructor via the vtable drop slot, then walks the
     * ancestor chain up to here. Override `~T()` in a subclass to release
     * resources deterministically at scope exit.
     *
     * The default body is **empty** — `Object` owns no resources. The
     * compiler-synthesized per-class drop wrapper still performs:
     *
     * 1. the class's own `~T()` (if any) + field auto-drops, then
     * 2. each ancestor's destructor in reverse declaration order, then
     * 3. a single `__cajeta_free` of the instance.
     *
     * ## Debugging
     *
     * An inherited or overridden destructor body carries normal statement
     * safepoints under `--debug-info`, so **setting a breakpoint on a class's
     * `~T()` is the supported way to answer _"when was this instance
     * destructed?"_** — an ordinary source breakpoint, no special machinery.
     *
     * @See docs/specification/lang/MemoryModel.md § Destructors
     */
    ~Object() {
    }
}
